name: Chatwoot work session - audit the conversation-record contract before a rework
repo: chatwoot
contract_symbol: Conversation
contract_file: app/models/conversation.rb
# TWO-STEP PROBE SCENARIO (plans/01-author.md), CYCLE 1 on this repo - a FIRST DRAFT, not
# a re-question. It exists so the next phase can run the mini-bench - both arms, once, at
# the real wall, unscored - against a real baseline. Nothing here is a verdict: no delta
# is claimed and none has been measured on this repo, ever.
#
# Single-prompt session bench (bench/drivers/bench-sense-local.sh): every step is presented
# in ONE prompt and worked in order. Rendered to the agent: the description + each step's
# name and prompt ONLY (checks and gold are NEVER shown). Everything rendered is kept
# NEUTRAL - it states the task, never the answer's shape, file paths, class or method
# names, counts, or which tool to reach for.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO TRAJECTORY - THIS REPO HAS NEVER BEEN RUN
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   ls verticals/ruby-rails/results/loop/chatwoot/
#     author.agent.json  author.agent.log      (both zero bytes - this run's own files)
#   ls verticals/ruby-rails/results/dryrun/chatwoot/
#     (empty)
# There is no cycles.jsonl, no minibench.md and no scenario .bak on this repo, so there is
# no window side to correct from and no previous cycle to read. Everything below that is
# carried in comes from the BANKED cells on the other three repos of this vertical, quoted
# under WHAT IS BEING REPRODUCED.
#
#   python3 bench/lib/inverse_frequency.py chatwoot .../claude-opus-5/validation
#   has nothing to say here: every run under that root is a mastodon or discourse or rails
#   run, and no run on disk has ever scored a chatwoot gold row. The free / hard /
#   discriminating classification therefore did NOT cut or keep a single row below, and no
#   later cycle should mistake its absence for evidence about these rows.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS BEING REPRODUCED - the measured recipe, quoted from banked.jsonl
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   mastodon  dependents  baseline 4/20, 5/20 (mean 0.225)  sense 20/20, 20/20 (1.000)  +0.775
#   discourse dependents  baseline 4/12, 1/12 (mean 0.208)  sense 10/12, 12/12 (0.917)  +0.708
#   rails     dependents  baseline 5/9, 3/9, 3/9 (0.407)    sense 9/9, 9/9, 8/9 (0.963)  +0.556
# All three are the SAME shape: a central Rails class as the anchor, a teardown audit as
# the ask, a narrow non-scoring step 1, and a scored `dependents` group of scattered
# one-per-file periphery rows. The mastodon cell is the closest match - same vertical, same
# model, same two-step probe expanded - and its axis is the one copied here, stated below.
# This draft does not invent a new lever; it applies the vertical's three-for-three recipe
# to a repo it has not been tried on.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE AXIS
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE ANSWER IS, THAT IS NOT A LIST OF THE PLACES A NAME APPEARS:
#   for each dependent, the ENCLOSING ROUTINE the dependency sits inside - the method, or
#   the class-body position, that owns the line and whose behaviour actually changes - plus
#   what that routine's own job starts getting wrong. A search prints the matching line,
#   its path and its number; it never prints the definition that encloses the match. To
#   name the enclosing routine you have to open the file and read upward, or ask something
#   that already holds the structure.
#
# That is an asymmetry in this instrument's payload on THIS anchor, not a wording trick.
# Quoted from the mcp_probe output recorded under SEAM PROFILE below, the shown blast set
# gives the enclosing routine on every row for free:
#     {"symbol":"AutomationRules::ConditionsFilterService#base_relation", ...}
#     {"symbol":"Captain::Tools::Copilot::SearchConversationsService#valid_status?", ...}
#     {"symbol":"ChatwootHub.instance_metrics", ...}
# and `grep -rn '\bConversation\b'` gives, for the same three rows:
#     app/services/automation_rules/conditions_filter_service.rb:188:    records = Conversation.where(id: @conversation.id).joins(
#     enterprise/app/services/captain/tools/copilot/search_conversations_service.rb:44:    status.present? && Conversation.statuses.key?(status)
#     lib/chatwoot_hub.rb:74:      conversations_count: fetch_count(Conversation),
# Not one of the three grep lines contains the name of the routine it is inside.
#
# THE MECHANISM SENTENCE, which the next phase measures: a dependent reaches this record
# class once, in the middle of doing some other job - rolling up an instance metric,
# validating a saved filter operand, claiming a row for an auto-assignment, unfurling a
# chat link, deciding whether a bot may still reply - and the line that takes the
# dependency does not say whose behaviour changes when the contract moves.
#
# THE HONEST RISK, stated before the run, per QUOTE IT OR YOU HAVE NOT VERIFIED IT:
#  a. The scorer cannot enforce the enclosing-routine demand. `bench/lib/gold.py:_cited`
#     credits a gold path followed by ANY line number and never compares it to gold's, so
#     an arm that dumps grep output and omits every routine name still scores the row. The
#     bet is behavioural, and it is the same bet the mastodon cell won on. Only the judge's
#     map_quality and specificity see the demand directly.
#  b. WEAKER THAN MASTODON ON ONE POINT, and it is checked, not assumed. Mastodon's axis
#     leaned on the enclosing routine being named after some OTHER job. Here that holds for
#     eleven of the eighteen scored rows - `base_relation`, `claim_and_assign`,
#     `valid_priorities`, `valid_status?`, `unread_since_last_seen_condition`,
#     `instance_metrics`, `index`, `filter_unassigned_and_mine`, `perform`,
#     `user_can_access_conversation?`, the `Notification` class body - and NOT for the
#     other seven, whose routine names do echo the contract's own word
#     (`conversation_status_values`, `merge_conversations`, `find_conversation_by_id`,
#     `conversation`, `set_conversation`, `conversation_pending?`,
#     `build_orphan_conversations_query`). Every routine name above was read out of the
#     file with an enclosing-def scan, not guessed. So the ask below demands the enclosing
#     routine AND the consequence for that routine's own job; the consequence is the part
#     that holds on all eighteen.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEAM PROFILE - recorded, never a gate
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   python3 bench/lib/seam_hunt.py $CLONE Conversation --file app/models/conversation.rb
#     app caller files : 59   (total incl test/non-app: 61)
#     SCATTER (dirs)   : 40
#     GREP-NOISE       : 'Conversation' in 61 app files   PRECISION=0.97
#     VERDICT          : SCATTERED + grep-clean
#   PRECISION=0.97 is the instrument's number and it is recorded as such. It is INFLATED on
#   this repo by a known denominator gap: seam_hunt's ruby row greps `app` and `lib` only
#   (bench/lib/seam_hunt.py:51), and chatwoot keeps a whole second application tree under
#   `enterprise/`. Counting the tree the arms actually see -
#     grep -rlw --include='*.rb' Conversation app lib enterprise db config | wc -l  ->  87
#   gives 59/87 = 0.68. Both numbers are stated; neither one decided anything. Per
#   PRECISION RANKS, IT NEVER KILLS, it was used only to order the pool, and the run decides.
#
#   The two alternates profiled, same command, same corrected denominator:
#     Inbox   (app/models/inbox.rb)   25 callers / 21 dirs / 35 grep files  prec 0.71
#     Contact (app/models/contact.rb) 31 callers / 23 dirs / 43 grep files  prec 0.72
#   Both are ranked better than 0.97 on the instrument's own scale and worse than
#   Conversation's on scatter (21 and 23 dirs against 40), and both concentrate: Inbox puts
#   9 of its 34 shown callers in one seeder file and 5 more in one jobs directory, Contact
#   puts 6 of 31 in `app/services/contacts`. Conversation is taken as the repo's central
#   contract - the record the whole product hangs off - with the widest scatter of the three.
#
#   python3 bench/lib/mcp_probe.py $CLONE '[{"name":"sense_blast","arguments":
#     {"symbol":"Conversation","file":"app/models/conversation.rb"}}]'
#     "risk":"high","risk_factors":["60 direct callers"]
#     "affected_symbols":271,"affected_files":88,"production_affected":97,"test_affected":36
#     "direct_callers_by_area": 40 areas
#     "affected_via_composition": 17 rows   "affected_via_includes":[]  "affected_subclasses":[]
#   THE ANCHOR IS AMBIGUOUS OVER MCP, and the sense arm pays one extra call for it. With no
#   `file` argument the same probe returns, verbatim:
#     {"ambiguous":true,"matches":4,"hint":"Multiple symbols named \"Conversation\" exist -
#      retry this tool with the `file` parameter ... e.g. file: \"app/models/conversation.rb\"",
#      "top_matches":["Conversation (136 edges, class) app/models/conversation.rb:54",
#                     "Enterprise::Concerns::Conversation ...", ...]}
#   The hint names the contract file as its own example and lists it first at 136 edges, so
#   the retry is mechanical, not a dead end. Recorded because it makes the sense arm's first
#   blast cost two calls instead of one, which is an efficiency-axis fact the next phase
#   will see in the transcripts.
#     python3 bench/lib/gold_confidence_check.py $YAML Conversation --group dependents
#       gold rows: 18   shown: 0    FAIL      <- the ambiguous, no-`file` call
#     ... --file app/models/conversation.rb --group dependents
#       gold rows: 18   shown: 18   PASS      <- the call the arm makes after the hint
#   Both are reported; the second is the one the DONE WHEN gate is met on, because it is the
#   payload the arm is actually shown.
#
#   All 18 `dependents` rows below appear in that shown payload - 17 in `direct_callers`,
#   `app/models/notification.rb` among them. Checked one by one against the printed set.
#   TWO CANDIDATE ROWS WERE DROPPED FOR BEING FALSE EDGES, by hand, not by a census:
#   `app/models/user_session.rb` (payload pins `UserSession#should_update_activity?`) and
#   `enterprise/app/services/captain/llm/faq_generator_service.rb` (pins `#system_prompt`)
#   contain no reference to the contract anywhere in the file -
#     grep -n 'Conversation' app/models/user_session.rb   ->  (no output)
#   - so neither could be credited honestly and neither is golded. That is a real defect in
#   the resolver's caller edges on this anchor, named here per the scope-lock rule and not
#   acted on: the loop routes it, this phase does not.
#
#   python3 bench/lib/ring_sweep.py $CLONE --top 12
#     0 candidate(s) above the bar - all twelve anchors at rows 0, files 0, vias 0.
#   The ring is EMPTY, as it was on both Ruby repos previously swept, so the "what HOLDS X"
#   kind is off the menu here. This is KIND ONE in its shape - an occurrence-list question -
#   and the sentence under THE AXIS says what its ANSWER is that the occurrence list does
#   not contain. What makes this the banked sort rather than the five-failure sort: the
#   scored group is asked SECOND, after step 1 has already spent wall and context on a
#   different sub-surface, and step 1's own targets are all in the non-scoring group so
#   nothing it finds can be cashed in step 2.
#
#   MEMORIZATION: python3 bench/lib/memorization_probe.py $CLONE Conversation
#     "reason": "closed-book recall 0.194 < 0.3 - not recited", samples 0.194 / 0.0 / 0.0
#     hits (7 of 36): cached_label_list_array, last_activity_at, last_incoming_message,
#                     recent_messages, toggle_status, unread_incoming_messages, unread_messages
#   What comes back recited is the contract's own MEMBER NAMES. The probe named no dependent
#   file and no file:line for anything; no gold row below is a member of the class - every
#   one is a third-party file citing it.
description: |
  You are a maintainer about to rework the record that stands for a single back-and-forth
  between a customer and the support team - the one the whole product is organised around,
  that the rest of the system keeps having to ask about. The rework will change what that
  record hands back to the code that asks it questions. Before you touch it you have to
  know two things: how the system gets hold of the right one of these when a reply arrives
  from outside and all it holds are the headers that came with it, and everywhere else in
  the codebase that would have to change with it. Throughout, an answer that points at a
  place must say which routine that place is inside, and give an exact file:line a teammate
  could jump straight to, plus one phrase on why it matters. A bare filename does not
  count, a file:line with no routine named around it does not count, and a missed dependent
  is a regression shipped.
steps:
- name: Map the conversation-record contract
  prompt: "Task: orient, and be thorough - the rest of the ticket is built on this. Start
    with the one part of this record's job that the whole inbound-mail side leans on:
    getting hold of the right one of these when a message arrives from outside and all the
    system holds are the headers that came with it. Trace that path end to end. Show where
    an arriving message is decided to belong to this path at all, the piece that runs the
    candidate matches in order, each separate way a match is attempted and what identifier
    it keys off, the fallback that starts a fresh record when none of them matched, and the
    point where a record that was only prepared is finally made permanent. For each one
    give the file:line, the name of the routine it lives in, and one phrase on what it
    contributes to that path. Then say, in a few sentences, what a rework would have to
    preserve for that path to keep working. A place named without the routine it lives in
    does not count, and neither does a routine named without the file:line where it is
    defined.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '8'
    required: true
    description: Inbound matching path traced through the routines that make it up, each located
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_graph
    required: false
    layer: adoption
- name: Audit every dependent of the conversation-record contract
  prompt: "Task: this is the core of the ticket, and you are working it with the budget the
    first part already cost you. Widen back out from that one path to the record class as a
    whole, as a single subject, and find EVERY place in the codebase that would have to
    change with it, so the rework cannot silently break one. Be exhaustive and group them
    by area. Most of what you are looking for is not doing this record's job at all: it is
    in the middle of some other job entirely - counting something for a dashboard, checking
    a stored filter operand, claiming a row so two background runs cannot collide,
    rendering a preview for a chat integration, deciding whether an automated replier may
    still speak - and it reaches for this class exactly once to settle a question that
    other job needs answered. So the answer you owe is not where the dependency sits but
    WHOSE behaviour changes: for every dependent, name the routine the dependency is
    inside, give its file:line, and say in one phrase what that routine would start getting
    wrong if the class stopped handing back what it hands back today. A file:line with no
    routine named around it does not count, a routine you cannot state the consequence for
    does not count, and a missed dependent is a regression shipped.
    "
  checks:
  - type: response_richness
    value: '6'
    required: true
    description: Dependents attributed to the enclosing routine, with the consequence named
  - type: no_grep
    value: no_grep
    required: false
    layer: adoption
  - type: mcp_tool_used
    value: sense_blast
    required: false
    layer: adoption
scoring:
  weights:
    correctness: 0.70
    efficiency: 0.30
# Gold is the must-find audit of what depends on the conversation record. TWO groups:
#   - `contract` (9, NON-SCORING anchors): the class file and the eight files that make up
#     the inbound-mail matching path. These are step 1's targets. Both arms reach them and
#     they do NOT score; they are here so step 1's cost is charged without step 1 handing
#     anything to step 2, and so no file step 1 leads to can be cashed in the scored group.
#   - `dependents` (18, THE DISCRIMINATOR): one row per FILE, across 17 distinct
#     directories in three trees (`app`, `lib`, `enterprise`). Not one of them is on the
#     matching path step 1 traces. Every row appears in the shown blast payload quoted in
#     the header, and every line below was opened and read by hand - the payload pins the
#     enclosing definition, not the dependency line, and the two differ on several rows
#     (`app/models/notification.rb` most sharply: the payload pins line 196, the dependency
#     is the class-body constant on line 56).
# Stated plainly, so no later cycle has to rediscover it: all 18 files contain the literal
# token `Conversation`, and one `grep -rn` returns all 18 paths with line numbers. That is
# forced - plan step 3 requires gold to come from the shown payload, and this payload's
# `direct_callers` rows are all `"relation":"calls Conversation"` - and per
# TOKEN-DARKNESS IS NOT THE MECHANISM the banked cells of this vertical did not need
# darkness either. The discriminator this draft rests on is the enclosing routine and the
# consequence for its own job, which grep's output carries for none of the 18.
gold:
- {id: contract-model, group: contract, match: [app/models/conversation.rb], relation: "app/models/conversation.rb:54 `class Conversation < ApplicationRecord` - the contract under rework; lines 55-63 mix in the nine concerns its behaviour is assembled from"}
- {id: c:application-mailbox, group: contract, match: [app/mailboxes/application_mailbox.rb], relation: "app/mailboxes/application_mailbox.rb:10 `routing(lambda { |inbound_mail| valid_to_address?(inbound_mail) && (reply_uuid_mail?(inbound_mail) || EmailChannelFinder.new(inbound_mail.mail).perform.present?) } => :reply)` - the front door that decides an arriving mail belongs to this path at all"}
- {id: c:reply-mailbox, group: contract, match: [app/mailboxes/reply_mailbox.rb], relation: "app/mailboxes/reply_mailbox.rb:24 `@conversation = Mailbox::ConversationFinder.new(mail).find` - inside `ReplyMailbox#find_conversation`, the before_processing hook every inbound reply enters through"}
- {id: c:finder, group: contract, match: [app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder.rb], relation: "app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder.rb:15 `@strategies.each do |strategy_class|` - inside `Mailbox::ConversationFinder#find`, the piece that runs the four candidate matches in the order declared at line 2 and returns the first that hits"}
- {id: c:base-strategy, group: contract, match: [app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder_strategies/base_strategy.rb], relation: "app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder_strategies/base_strategy.rb:12 `def find` raising `NotImplementedError` - the contract every match attempt implements, and the comment above it declares the return type as the record or nil"}
- {id: c:receiver-uuid, group: contract, match: [app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder_strategies/receiver_uuid_strategy.rb], relation: "app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder_strategies/receiver_uuid_strategy.rb:9 `Conversation.find_by(uuid: uuid)` - inside `#find`, the first attempt; it keys off the uuid embedded in the reply-to address by the pattern on line 3"}
- {id: c:in-reply-to, group: contract, match: [app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder_strategies/in_reply_to_strategy.rb], relation: "app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder_strategies/in_reply_to_strategy.rb:23 `conversation = Conversation.find_by(uuid: uuid)` - inside `#find`, the second attempt; it keys off the In-Reply-To header, and line 29 falls back to the record hanging off a matched message"}
- {id: c:references, group: contract, match: [app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder_strategies/references_strategy.rb], relation: "app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder_strategies/references_strategy.rb:41 `conversation = Conversation.find_by(uuid: uuid, inbox_id: @channel.inbox.id)` - inside `#find_conversation_from_reference`, the third attempt; it walks the References header and scopes the match to the channel's inbox"}
- {id: c:new-conversation, group: contract, match: [app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder_strategies/new_conversation_strategy.rb], relation: "app/services/mailbox/conversation_finder_strategies/new_conversation_strategy.rb:57 `@conversation = ::Conversation.new(account_id: @account.id, inbox_id: @inbox.id, ...)` - inside `#build_conversation`, the fallback that PREPARES an unsaved record; `ReplyMailbox#persist_conversation_if_needed` is what makes it permanent"}
- {id: d:hub-instance-metrics, group: dependents, match: [lib/chatwoot_hub.rb], relation: "lib/chatwoot_hub.rb:74 `conversations_count: fetch_count(Conversation)` - inside `ChatwootHub.instance_metrics`, whose job is the telemetry payload sent home; the class is one counted term among several"}
- {id: d:filter-status-values, group: dependents, match: [app/helpers/filters/filter_helper.rb], relation: "app/helpers/filters/filter_helper.rb:98 `return Conversation.statuses.values if values.include?('all')` - inside `#conversation_status_values`, whose job is casting a saved filter's operand; it reads the enum mapping off the class to do it"}
- {id: d:automation-base-relation, group: dependents, match: [app/services/automation_rules/conditions_filter_service.rb], relation: "app/services/automation_rules/conditions_filter_service.rb:188 `records = Conversation.where(id: @conversation.id).joins(` - inside `#base_relation`, whose job is building the query an automation rule's conditions are evaluated against"}
- {id: d:auto-assign-claim, group: dependents, match: [app/services/auto_assignment/assignment_service.rb], relation: "app/services/auto_assignment/assignment_service.rb:93 `Conversation.transaction do` - inside `#claim_and_assign`, whose job is stopping two overlapping bulk runs from assigning the same row twice; it opens the transaction on the class"}
- {id: d:unread-counts-arel, group: dependents, match: [app/services/conversations/unread_counts/builder.rb], relation: "app/services/conversations/unread_counts/builder.rb:57 `conversations = Conversation.arel_table` - inside `#unread_since_last_seen_condition`, whose job is the unread badge figure; it reaches through to the class's arel table to write the comparison"}
- {id: d:orphan-sweep-scope, group: dependents, match: [app/services/internal/remove_orphan_conversations_service.rb], relation: "app/services/internal/remove_orphan_conversations_service.rb:27 `base = @account ? @account.conversations : Conversation.all` - inside `#build_orphan_conversations_query`, whose job is the housekeeping sweep; the class scope is what it falls back to when no account narrows it"}
- {id: d:contact-merge, group: dependents, match: [app/actions/contact_merge_action.rb], relation: "app/actions/contact_merge_action.rb:34 `Conversation.where(contact_id: @mergee_contact.id).update(contact_id: @base_contact.id)` - inside `#merge_conversations`, whose job is folding one customer record into another; it repoints the rows in bulk"}
- {id: d:notification-primary-actor, group: dependents, match: [app/models/notification.rb], relation: "app/models/notification.rb:56 `PRIMARY_ACTORS = ['Conversation'].freeze` - in the `Notification` class body, whose job is the notification record; it names the class as a STRING, so the dependency is a polymorphic type name and not a constant reference"}
- {id: d:notification-builder-access, group: dependents, match: [app/builders/notification_builder.rb], relation: "app/builders/notification_builder.rb:43 `conversation = primary_actor.is_a?(Conversation) ? primary_actor : primary_actor.try(:conversation)` - inside `#user_can_access_conversation?`, whose job is suppressing a notification the recipient may not see; it type-tests the polymorphic actor"}
- {id: d:reply-email-job, group: dependents, match: [app/jobs/conversation_reply_email_job.rb], relation: "app/jobs/conversation_reply_email_job.rb:5 `conversation = Conversation.find(conversation_id)` - inside `#perform`, whose job is sending the batched email reply; it is handed an id, not a record, so it re-looks it up in the background"}
- {id: d:super-admin-dashboard, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/super_admin/dashboard_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/super_admin/dashboard_controller.rb:5 `@data = Conversation.unscoped.group_by_day(:created_at, range: 30.days.ago..2.seconds.ago).count.to_a` - inside `#index`, whose job is the installation-wide admin chart; it goes UNSCOPED, deliberately outside account scoping"}
- {id: d:csat-public-lookup, group: dependents, match: [app/controllers/public/api/v1/csat_survey_controller.rb], relation: "app/controllers/public/api/v1/csat_survey_controller.rb:18 `@conversation = Conversation.find_by!(uuid: params[:id])` - inside `#set_conversation`, whose job is serving the unauthenticated survey page; the uuid is the only credential the request carries"}
- {id: d:slack-thread-lookup, group: dependents, match: [lib/integrations/slack/slack_message_helper.rb], relation: "lib/integrations/slack/slack_message_helper.rb:86 `@conversation ||= Conversation.where(identifier: params[:event][:thread_ts]).first` - inside `#conversation`, whose job is routing a reply typed in a chat thread back into the product; it keys off the thread timestamp stored on the record"}
- {id: d:slack-unfurl, group: dependents, match: [lib/integrations/slack/slack_link_unfurl_service.rb], relation: "lib/integrations/slack/slack_link_unfurl_service.rb:65 `Conversation.find_by(display_id: conversation_id, account_id: integration_hook.account_id)` - inside `#find_conversation_by_id`, whose job is rendering a link preview in a chat message; it resolves the per-account display id, not the primary key"}
- {id: d:enterprise-permission-filter, group: dependents, match: [enterprise/app/services/enterprise/conversations/permission_filter_service.rb], relation: "enterprise/app/services/enterprise/conversations/permission_filter_service.rb:36 `Conversation.from(\"(#{mine.to_sql} UNION #{unassigned.to_sql}) as conversations\")` - inside `#filter_unassigned_and_mine`, whose job is the restricted-agent view; it re-wraps two subqueries as the class so the caller still gets a relation"}
- {id: d:captain-pending-check, group: dependents, match: [enterprise/app/jobs/captain/conversation/response_builder_job.rb], relation: "enterprise/app/jobs/captain/conversation/response_builder_job.rb:215 `status = Conversation.uncached { Conversation.where(id: @conversation.id).pick(:status) }` - inside `#conversation_pending?`, whose job is deciding whether the automated replier may still speak; it bypasses the query cache to re-read the status"}
- {id: d:captain-search-status, group: dependents, match: [enterprise/app/services/captain/tools/copilot/search_conversations_service.rb], relation: "enterprise/app/services/captain/tools/copilot/search_conversations_service.rb:44 `status.present? && Conversation.statuses.key?(status)` - inside `#valid_status?`, whose job is validating an argument the assistant supplied; the enum mapping is the whitelist"}
